    # No part of this cabinet collides with any other part of it. The carcass, the
    # four doors and the four drawers are modelled flush against each other, and
    # their convex approximations overlap by a few millimetres -- enough that a
    # drawer scrapes its neighbours and the carcass on the way out, or gets stuck
    # against them. What holds a drawer in its slot is its joint limit, not
    # contact, so nothing is lost by filtering these pairs away.
    #
    # A collision group that names *itself* in physics:filteredGroups filters
    # every pair inside the group and only those: the gripper, the robot and the
    # rest of the scene still collide with the handles and panels as before.
    # Including the root prim covers every collider under it (a collection
    # expands to descendants), so parts added later are filtered too.
    def PhysicsCollisionGroup "cabinet_parts"
    {
        prepend rel collection:colliders:includes = </cabinet_A71cf8c99Bd8a4ab0847b101e3f6f9fa4>
        prepend rel physics:filteredGroups = </cabinet_A71cf8c99Bd8a4ab0847b101e3f6f9fa4/cabinet_parts>
    }

    # Every hinge and slide carries a drive with stiffness 0, target velocity 0 and
    # damping: no spring pulling the door anywhere, just resistance proportional to
    # speed -- which is what the friction in a real hinge or drawer runner does. A
    # frictionless joint never stops once something touches it, and a door still
    # drifting when the gripper closes again is a door that chatters against it.
    # Damping is acceleration-normalised, so the number is independent of the body's
    # mass; angular drives measure velocity in deg/s, which is why a hinge wants a
    # much smaller number than a slide. Measured at these values: a 25 N shove now
    # coasts 6-9 mm instead of 180-470 mm and stops dead, while a 25 N pull still
    # swings a door 0.5-0.8 m and draws a drawer 0.19 m out in three seconds.
